const faqs = [
  { q: "Is the Chinese zodiac based on your birthday or your birth year?", a: "Mainly your birth year — specifically the lunar year you were born in. Unlike Western astrology, which is tied to the month and the position of the stars, the Chinese zodiac assigns one animal to each whole year in a repeating 12-year cycle." },
  { q: "Does the Chinese zodiac year start on January 1?", a: "No. It starts on Lunar New Year, which falls between late January and mid-February. So someone born in early February might belong to the previous year's animal. The 2026 Horse year begins on February 17, 2026." },
  { q: "What animal is 2026 in the Chinese zodiac?", a: "2026 is the Year of the Horse 马 — more precisely the Fire Horse (丙午). It begins on February 17, 2026 and runs to early February 2027." },
  { q: "Is the Chinese zodiac fortune telling?", a: "Not in the strict sense. Traditionally it's used for personality association, year symbolism, and family conversation — not as a guaranteed prediction of your future. We write about it as culture, not as a promise about your life." },
  { q: "What are the 12 zodiac animals in order?", a: "Rat, Ox, Tiger, Rabbit, Dragon, Snake, Horse, Goat, Monkey, Rooster, Dog and Pig — 鼠牛虎兔龙蛇马羊猴鸡狗猪." },
  { q: "Why is there no cat in the Chinese zodiac?", a: "A famous legend says the animals raced to decide the order. The Rat tricked the Cat into missing the race (in some versions, by not waking it), which is why the Cat never made the twelve — and why cats and rats are said to be enemies ever since." },
  { q: "What is Ben Ming Nian (本命年)?", a: "It's the year of your own zodiac animal, which comes around every 12 years. It's traditionally seen as a year to be careful, and many people wear red — red underwear, a red belt or a red bracelet — for protection and luck." },
  { q: "How is the Chinese zodiac different from Western astrology?", a: "Western astrology is monthly and star-based (12 signs across one year). The Chinese zodiac is yearly and animal-based (12 animals across 12 years). They are not the same system and don't map onto each other." },
  { q: "What does my zodiac animal say about compatibility?", a: "Tradition pairs certain animals as more or less harmonious, often used lightly when families talk about relationships. We treat this as cultural color, not relationship advice." },
  { q: "Are these traits true about everyone born in that year?", a: "No — they're cultural associations, not rules. In Chinese tradition people born in a given animal's year are often associated with certain qualities, but it's a way of talking about character, not a fixed destiny." },
];
