<section class="sec">
  <h2 id="myth">Don't read it like a Western horoscope</h2>
  <div class="note"><span class="nlbl">Native note</span><b>It's yearly, not monthly</b> — There is no "what's my Chinese star sign by birthday." Your animal is set by the whole lunar year, full stop. If a site asks for your exact date, it's usually just to check which side of Lunar New Year you fall on.</p></div>
  <div class="note"><span class="nlbl">Native note</span><b>Animals, not constellations</b> — Western zodiac signs are patterns of stars. Chinese zodiac animals are earthly creatures tied to a counting system for years. Different logic, different history — they don't translate onto each other.</p></div>
  <div class="note"><span class="nlbl">Native note</span><b>Color, not command</b> — The traits here are how the culture <i>talks about</i> a sign, not a verdict on who you are. A Tiger can be shy; a Rabbit can be fierce. Treat it like the stories it comes from: meaningful, beloved, and not to be taken as literal fate.</p></div>
</section>

